ls":-0.010497741477251387,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":64.9,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.0699315778097099,"_gp_rev_volatility":true,"_score_rev_volatility":94.3,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":0.06534582491556223,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":false,"_score_margin_vs_hist":18.3,"_gate_piotroski":5,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":55.6,"_gate_spread":null,"_gp_spread":null,"_score_spread":null,"_gate_roic_consistency":null,"_gp_roic_consistency":null,"_score_roic_consistency":null,"_gate_incr_roic":null,"_gp_incr_roic":null,"_score_incr_roic":null,"_gate_margin_advantage":null,"_gp_margin_advantage":null,"_score_margin_advantage":null,"_gate_pool_share":0.061519055634222886,"_gp_pool_share":true,"_score_pool_share":64.6,"_gate_rev_durability":0.10320877213250323,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":76.6,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.04354600134767844,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":59.5,"_gate_share_shrink":0.010601263069934141,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":36.7,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.025837281980259103,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":56.9,"_gate_insider_own":0.03206,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":21.4,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Stifel Financial Corp. operates as the bank holding company for Stifel, Nicolaus & Company, Incorporated that provides retail and institutional wealth management, and investment banking services to individual, corporations, municipalities, and institutions in the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada, and internationally. It operates in three segments: Global Wealth Management, Institutional Group, and Other. The company provides private client services, including securities transaction and financial planning services; securities brokerage services, such as the sale of equities, mutual funds, fixed income products, and insurance; institutional equity and fixed income sales, trading and research, and municipal finance services; investment banking services, such as mergers and acquisitions, public offerings, and private placements; and retail and commercial banking services comprising personal and commercial lending programs, as well as deposit accounts. It participates in and manages underwritings for corporate and public finance; and offers financial advisory and securities brokerage services. The company was founded in 1890 and is headquartered in Saint Louis, Missouri.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Ronald James Kruszewski | Chairman & CEO | Age 66 | Compensation: $10.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 8K people. Revenue per employee of $713K ranks in the above the sector median among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 11%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $119K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $18K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 14\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Stifel Financial Corporation is a large-cap financial services business with a $10.3B market cap on $6.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +11% over the past three years and +10% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 24% against a 10% cost of capital \u2014 a 14-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $1.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 17 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.63\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 2.1\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 4.4% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 3.2% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.5) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 40% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Is Stifel Financial (NYSE:SF) Building A Steadier Dividend Story?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.co